// draw axes
draw((-3, 0)--(15, 0), Arrow); draw((0, -3)--(0, 15), Arrow);
label("$y$", (0, 15), N); label("$x$", (15, 0), E);

// draw line and T dot
draw((-1, 9 + 3/4)--(40/3, -1));
dot((8, 3));

// Add labels
label("$O$", (0, 0), SW); label("$Q$", (0, 9), NE); label("$P$", (12, 0), NE);
label("$T(r, s)$", (8, 3), NE);

[/asy] -------------------- 
[92mGround Truth:[0m
11 -------------------- 
[94mResponse:[0m
The area of the triangle $OPQ$ is given by the formula $\frac{1}{2} \times OP \times OQ$. Here, $OP = 12$ and $OQ = 9$. So, the area of $\triangle OPQ$ is $\frac{1}{2} \times 12 \times 9 = 54$.

The area of the triangle $TOP$ is given by the formula $\frac{1}{2} \times TO \times OP$. Here, $TO = 3$ and $OP = 12$. So, the area of $\triangle TOP$ is $\frac{1}{2} \times 3 \times 12 = 18$.

Given that the area of $\triangle OPQ$ is three times the area of $\triangle TOP$, we have $54 = 3 \times 18$, which is true.

Therefore, the value of $r + s$ is $8 + 3 = 11$.

So, the value of $r + s$ is $\boxed{11}$.
